    "guaCiZh": "元亨利貞",
    "imageCommentaryEn": "Heaven in its motion gives the idea of strength. The superior person, in accordance with this, nerves himself to ceaseless activity.",
    "imageCommentaryZh": "天行健，君子以自強不息。",
    "overviewEn": "Hexagram 1 — The Creative (☰☰) is the very first of the 64 hexagrams of the I Ching. Formed by doubling the trigram The Creative (☰), all six lines are yang — making it the only hexagram of pure yang among the sixty-four. The character 乾 (qián) originally means 'rising upward,' extending to signify the vastness and vigor of heaven — the ceaseless motion of the celestial way.\nJudgment: Sublime Success. Perseverance Furthers.\nThe four words of the Judgment — Yuan, Heng, Li, Zhen (元亨利貞) — form the overarching principle of Hexagram 1, and indeed the most essential four virtues of the entire I Ching. The Wenyan Commentary elaborates: 'Yuan is the origin of all goodness; Heng is the convergence of all beauty; Li is the harmony of all righteousness; Zhen is the backbone of all endeavor.'\nYuan (Sublimity): The origin of all things, the root of creation — representing early spring, new life, and the beginning of every good thing. The noble person embodies benevolence and thus may lead others.\nHeng (Success): Free and unimpeded flow, like spring flowers in full bloom — representing career prosperity and harmonious relationships.\nLi (Furtherance): Appropriate benefit aligned with principle, contributing to the greater good without violating the way of heaven — representing harvest and achievement.\nZhen (Perseverance): Steadfast correctness, holding firm to the right path — the foundation upon which all lasting accomplishment is built.\nTogether, the four virtues mean: at the right moment (Yuan), in a spirit of openness (Heng), take beneficial action (Li), and always remain on the correct path (Zhen) — this is the complete guidance Hexagram 1 offers for life.\nTuan Commentary: Great Indeed Is the Sublimity of the Creative\nThe Tuan Commentary states: 'Great indeed is the sublimity of the Creative, to which all beings owe their beginning and which permeates all heaven. The clouds pass and the rain does its work, and all individual beings flow into their forms. Because the sage comprehends the nature of the end and the beginning, the six stages complete themselves in their own time. At the proper time he mounts the six dragons and thus rides across the sky. The way of the Creative works through change and transformation, so that each thing receives its true nature and destiny, and they are kept in accord with the Great Harmony. Thus does it further through perseverance. The sage towers above the multitude of beings, and all lands are united in peace.'\nThis passage reveals the cosmology of the hexagram: heaven is the source from which all things spring. Riding the six dragons (the six lines) across heaven and earth, each being finds its place and fulfills its destiny, and the world attains universal harmony. 'At the proper time he mounts the six dragons and rides across the sky' — this is the arc of the six lines: the dragon in its six phases from concealment to flight, mapping the complete journey of a life from dormancy to full ascent.\nImage: Heaven in Its Motion Gives the Idea of Strength. The Superior Person, in Accordance With This, Nerves Himself to Ceaseless Activity.\nThis is one of the most celebrated maxims of Chinese civilization. The motion of heaven is powerful and unceasing — sun, moon, and stars never pause. Emulating this celestial way, the noble person commits to tireless self-strengthening. 'Strength' (健) is the core virtue of the Creative — not brute force, but sustained, resilient, inexhaustible inner drive. This phrase has been inscribed in countless schools, academies, and family precepts, inspiring generations of men and women of purpose to strive ever onward.",
    "overviewZh": "乾卦（☰☰），為《周易》六十四卦之首，卦序第一，由上下兩個乾卦重疊而成，六爻皆陽，是六十四卦中唯一的純陽之卦。「乾」字本義為「上出」，引申為天空的廣闊與乾燥，象徵天道的剛健執行，永無止息。\n卦辭：元亨利貞\n「元、亨、利、貞」四字是乾卦的總綱，也是整部《易經》最核心的四德。《文言傳》對此有精闢闡發：「元者，善之長也；亨者，嘉之會也；利者，義之和也；貞者，事之幹也。」\n元：萬物之始，創生的根源，代表初春、新生、一切好事的開端。君子體仁，足以成為眾人之長。\n亨：通達順利，如春花怒放，永珍更新，嘉美之事彙集，代表事業順遂、人際亨通。\n利：恰當有益，合乎義理，利於眾生，代表收穫與成果，不違天道地做出貢獻。\n貞：正固守一，堅守正道，以貞固之德駕馭萬事，是一切成就的根基。\n四德合一，意味著：在正確的時機（元），以通達的方式（亨），做出有益的行動（利），並始終保持正道（貞）——這便是乾卦給予人生最完整的指引。\n彖傳：大哉乾元\n《彖傳》雲：「大哉乾元，萬物資始，乃統天。雲行雨施，品物流形。大明終始，六位時成，時乘六龍以御天。乾道變化，各正性命，保合大和，乃利貞。首出庶物，萬國咸寧。」\n這段話揭示了乾卦的宇宙觀：天是萬物生髮的根源，駕馭六龍（六爻）執行於天地之間，使萬物各得其位，各安其命，天下因此達到普遍的和諧與安寧。「時乘六龍以御天」，正是乾卦六爻所象徵的六個生命階段——龍在不同時位的形態，完整對映了人從潛藏到騰飛的完整旅程。\n大象傳：天行健，君子以自強不息\n這是中華文明最著名的自強格言之一。天道健行，日月星辰永不停歇；效法天道，君子當以自強不息為志。「健」是乾卦的核心德性——不是蠻力，而是持續、堅韌、永不疲倦的內在驅動力。這句話刻於無數學校、書院、家訓之中，千百年來激勵著無數仁人志士奮發進取。",
